<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ten Commandments</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Decalogue</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ten Commandments, or Decalogue, are religious and ethical directives which according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> God gave to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. The biblical narrative tells that God revealed them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a> and gave Moses two stone tablets, which he broke on seeing the worship of a golden calf, then a second set, placed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a>. The text appears in three distinct versions, at Exodus 20, Deuteronomy 5 and the Ritual Decalogue of Exodus 34, divided into ten commandments in different ways by different traditions. They are the foundational core of Jewish law, and most Christian traditions regard them as divinely authoritative.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ten-commandments.jpg" alt="Ten Commandments" width="330" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photography by Ardon Bar Hama. Manuscript at the Bibliotheca Rosenthaliana, Amsterdam HS.ROS.PL.a-33. Colophon in the bottom right corner re · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0001_FL9694984.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The weekly day of rest, the <b>Ten Commandments</b>, the prophetic call for justice for the poor and the stranger and the vision of a world at peace have entered the moral language of much of humanity.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Münster’s Anabaptist kingdom punished breaches of the <b>Ten Commandments</b> with death, and Savonarola’s Florence burned books and works of art in the Bonfire of the Vanities before turning against him; he was hanged and burned in 1498.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Since 1549 Anglicans have normally sung or said it in English; the Book of Common Prayer of 1552 set it into a recitation of the <b>Ten Commandments</b>, and modern revisions have restored the choice of saying it without them.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kyrie">Kyrie</a></li></ul></section>
